.TH "vsearch-fastq_chars" "1" "April 29, 2026" "version 2.31.0" "vsearch manual"
.hy
.SH NAME
.PP
vsearch --fastq_chars \[em] analyze fastq files to identify the quality
encoding and the range of quality score values used
.SH SYNOPSIS
.PP
\f[B]vsearch\f[R] \f[B]--fastq_chars\f[R] \f[I]fastqfile\f[R]
[\f[I]options\f[R]]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.PP
The vsearch command \f[V]--fastq_chars\f[R] summarizes the number and
composition of sequences and quality strings contained in the input
fastq file (see below for an example).
Results are written to the \f[I]standard error\f[R] \f[V]stderr(3)\f[R],
and to \f[I]filename\f[R] if option \f[V]--log\f[R] \f[I]filename\f[R]
is used.
.PP
The command \f[V]--fastq_chars\f[R] tries to automatically detect the
quality offset (33 or 64) and the fastq format (Solexa, Illumina 1.3+,
Illumina 1.5+, Illumina 1.8+, or the original Sanger format) by
analyzing the range of observed quality score values.
In case of success, \f[V]--fastq_chars\f[R] suggests values for the
quality offset \f[V]--fastq_ascii\f[R] (33 or 64), as well as
\f[V]--fastq_qmin\f[R] and \f[V]--fastq_qmax\f[R] values that could be
used with other commands.
If the quality encoding is ambiguous, an offset of 33 is favored.
For example:
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
Qmin 45, Qmax 73, Range 29
Guess: -fastq_qmin 12 -fastq_qmax 40 -fastq_ascii 33
Guess: Original Sanger format (phred+33)
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
A file detected as Solexa is reported as
\f[V]solexa+64, not supported\f[R]: that format shares the offset 64
with Illumina 1.3+ but defines its scores differently, and vsearch has
only the Phred formula (see \f[V]vsearch-fastq(5)\f[R]).
The suggested \f[V]--fastq_qmin\f[R] and \f[V]--fastq_ascii\f[R] values
will read such a file, but the error probabilities derived from it will
be too large at low scores.
.PP
For each sequence symbol, \f[V]--fastq_chars\f[R] gives the number of
occurrences of the symbol, its relative frequency, and the number of
consecutive repetitions after the first occurrence in the longest run of
that symbol \[em] that is, the longest run length minus one, so a symbol
that never appears twice in a row scores 0 (lowercase symbols are
converted to uppercase).
This matches usearch\[cq]s MaxRun column, except that vsearch also
counts a run ending with the read.
For example:
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
Letter          N   Freq MaxRun
------ ---------- ------ ------
     A    9050221  25.8%     19
     C    5657659  16.1%      9
     G    8731373  24.9%     11
     T   11610889  33.1%     24
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
When \f[V]N\f[R] symbols are present, their row also reports the range
of quality values observed at \f[V]N\f[R] positions
(\f[V]Q=min..max\f[R]).
.PP
For each quality symbol, \f[V]--fastq_chars\f[R] gives the ASCII value
of the symbol (see \f[V]ascii(7)\f[R]), its relative frequency, and the
number of times a \f[I]k\f[R]-mer of that symbol appears at the end of
quality strings.
The length of the \f[I]k\f[R]-mer can be set with the option
\f[V]--fastq_tail\f[R] (4 by default).
For example:
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
Char  ASCII    Freq       Tails
----  -----  ------  ----------
 \[aq]-\[aq]     45    0.7%           8
 \[aq].\[aq]     46    1.7%           9
 \[aq]/\[aq]     47    2.8%        2997
 \[aq]0\[aq]     48    2.4%         221
 \[aq]1\[aq]     49    0.9%           0
 \[aq]2\[aq]     50    0.4%           0
 \[aq]3\[aq]     51    0.4%           0
 \[aq]4\[aq]     52    0.2%           0
 \[aq]5\[aq]     53    0.3%           0
 \[aq]6\[aq]     54    0.0%           0
 \[aq]9\[aq]     57    1.3%          12
 \[aq]:\[aq]     58    0.7%           0
 \[aq];\[aq]     59    1.3%           1
 \[aq]<\[aq]     60    0.6%           0
 \[aq]=\[aq]     61    0.2%           0
 \[aq]>\[aq]     62    0.3%           0
 \[aq]?\[aq]     63    0.6%           0
 \[aq]\[at]\[aq]     64    0.3%           0
 \[aq]A\[aq]     65    1.9%           0
 \[aq]B\[aq]     66    3.9%          91
 \[aq]C\[aq]     67    2.2%           0
 \[aq]D\[aq]     68    1.4%           0
 \[aq]E\[aq]     69    2.9%           0
 \[aq]F\[aq]     70   14.7%       24657
 \[aq]G\[aq]     71   23.5%        5890
 \[aq]H\[aq]     72   34.5%           9
 \[aq]I\[aq]     73    0.0%           0
\f[R]
.fi
.SH OPTIONS
.SS core options
.TP
\f[V]--fastq_tail\f[R] \f[I]positive non-null integer\f[R]
Count the number of times a series of identical symbols of length
\f[I]k\f[R] = \f[I]positive non-null integer\f[R], a \f[I]k\f[R]-mer,
appears at the end of quality strings.
By default, \f[I]k\f[R] = 4.
.SS secondary options
.TP
\f[V]--bzip2_decompress\f[R]
Specify that the \f[I]input pipe\f[R] is streaming data compressed using
Huffman coding.
See \f[V]bzip2(1)\f[R] for more details.
This option is required when compressed data arrives on standard input
through a pipe (`-'), where the format cannot be detected without
consuming the stream.
It is not needed when reading from a regular file compressed with bzip2,
nor when such a file is redirected to standard input: compression is
then detected automatically, and a contradicting option is ignored (with
a warning when the input is standard input).
Pipes other than standard input, such as shell process substitutions and
named FIFOs, are always read as uncompressed data; compressed data must
arrive on standard input or as a named file.
.TP
\f[V]--gzip_decompress\f[R]
Specify that the \f[I]input pipe\f[R] is streaming data compressed using
Lempel-Ziv coding.
See \f[V]gzip(1)\f[R] for more details.
This option is required when compressed data arrives on standard input
through a pipe (`-'), where the format cannot be detected without
consuming the stream.
It is not needed when reading from a regular file compressed with gzip,
nor when such a file is redirected to standard input: compression is
then detected automatically, and a contradicting option is ignored (with
a warning when the input is standard input).
Pipes other than standard input, such as shell process substitutions and
named FIFOs, are always read as uncompressed data; compressed data must
arrive on standard input or as a named file.
.TP
\f[V]--log\f[R] \f[I]filename\f[R]
Write messages to \f[I]filename\f[R].
Messages include program version, start and finish times, elapsed time,
amount of memory available, maximum amount of memory consumed, number of
cores and command line options, and if need be, command-specific
informational messages, warnings, and errors.
A copy of the statistics computed by \f[V]--fastq_chars\f[R] is also
recorded.
.TP
\f[V]--no_progress\f[R]
Suppress the gradually increasing progress indicator normally written to
the \f[I]standard error\f[R] \f[V]stderr(3)\f[R].
.TP
\f[V]--quiet\f[R]
Suppress messages to the \f[I]standard output\f[R] \f[V]stdout(3)\f[R]
and \f[I]standard error\f[R] \f[V]stderr(3)\f[R], except for warnings
and error messages.
.SS ignored options
.TP
\f[V]--threads\f[R] \f[I]positive non-null integer\f[R]
Command is not multithreaded, option has no effect (a warning is printed
when more than one thread is requested).
.SH EXAMPLES
.PP
Read from \f[I]input.fastq.gz\f[R], count series of 5 identical symbols
at the end of quality strings (\f[V]--fastq_tail 5\f[R]), do not write
to the standard error (\f[V]--quiet\f[R]), and write results to
\f[I]output.log\f[R] (\f[V]--log\f[R]):
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
vsearch \[rs]
    --fastq_chars input.fastq.gz \[rs]
    --fastq_tail 5 \[rs]
    --quiet \[rs]
    --log output.log
\f[R]
.fi
